## Demonstration of `printGM()`

### Using `printGM()` with `wsMed()`
The `wsMed()` function returns a list containing `sem_model`. `printGM()` can automatically extract and format the model equations.

```{r, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
library(wsMed)
data(example_data)
result1 <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1", "C1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2", "C2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2"
)
printGM(result1)
```

### Using printGM() with GenerateModel*()
If you already have preprocessed data, you can directly generate the SEM model syntax using GenerateModelPC() or other GenerateModel*() functions.

```{r, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
# Load necessary packages
library(wsMed)
# Load example data
data(example_data)

# Prepare data
prepared_data <- PrepareData(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1", "C1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2", "C2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2"
)

# Generate Model
sem_model <- GenerateModelPC(prepared_data)

# print Model equations
printGM(sem_model)
```

`printGM()` displays the unstandardized model equations; requesting
standardized estimates does not rewrite the SEM syntax. For standardized
estimates and plots, use `summary(result1, standardized = TRUE)` and
`plot(result1, standardized = TRUE)`. The same methods also support
conditional results when a moderator is present; see
[Standardized moderated mediation](StandardizedModeratedMediation.html).
